{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with precision, tension, and emotional depth. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie transforms what could have been a dry tech origin story into a gripping psychological drama about ambition, betrayal, and isolation. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing his brilliance, social awkwardness, and emotional detachment with uncanny accuracy. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a man driven by intellect and wounded by rejection.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally compelling. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, zuckerberg’s loyal friend turned adversary, conveying quiet heartbreak and moral conflict. jesse plemons and josh lawrence bring gravitas to their roles as the winklevoss twins, embodying privilege and righteous anger with chilling precision. jude law, as the slick attorney representing the winklevosses, adds a layer of moral ambiguity and courtroom theatrics that elevate the film’s narrative tension.\n\nsorkin’s screenplay is a marvel of rapid-fire dialogue, layered with wit, irony, and emotional subtext. every line feels purposeful, advancing both plot and character. the film’s structure—intercutting between two legal depositions and the events leading up to them—is brilliantly executed, creating a sense of inevitability and mounting dread. the pacing never falters, even during lengthy legal scenes, thanks to the sharp writing and fincher’s meticulous direction.\n\ntony gilbert’s cinematography is cold and sleek, mirroring the digital world the characters inhabit. the use of shadows, tight close-ups, and sterile interiors reinforces the emotional distance between people in an age of hyper-connectivity. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is revolutionary—a pulsating, ambient electronic soundscape that feels both futuristic and haunting, perfectly complementing the film’s themes of innovation and alienation.\n\nbeneath its sleek surface, the social network is a tragedy about the cost of success. it asks whether true connection is possible in a world built on code and competition. zuckerberg’s rise is portrayed not as a triumph, but as a lonely, hollow victory. the final scene, in which he refreshes his own facebook page, hoping for a response from someone he’s lost, is devastating in its simplicity.\n\nthe social network is more than a movie about facebook—it’s a portrait of modern america, where genius is rewarded but humanity is often discarded. it’s smart, stylish, and deeply human. even years after its release, it remains one of the most relevant and resonant films of the 21st century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014625910262111574}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -4.410734163684538e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00043156370520591736}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4582989513874054}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.252384752035141}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0010349161457270384}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.003972972277551889}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011789103882620111}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.1295183897018433}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.006728847976773977}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.5502323508262634}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.207999518257566e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00028951745480298996}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.48412784934043884}, {"token": " precision", "logprob": -0.9521429538726807}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " tension", "logprob": -0.49